BananaOS's speed work on Panda3DS for the RG DS Plus (Cortex-A55), from branch banana3ds.
PANDA3DS_BASELINE=1 in the environment runs upstream's code paths instead. Measured with tools/3ds-perf.

  - dynarmic: VFP stores from GPRs
  - dynarmic: no FPSR traffic per block
  - dynarmic: dead NZCV writes dropped
  - dynarmic: conditional block exits use the live host flags
  - panda3ds-bench --dump-jit: the JIT's code for a disassembler
  - GL: sample render-to-texture colour buffers directly
  - GL: textures the CPU or the transfer engine rewrites show up again
  - Immediate mode: one draw per run of triangles (not per triangle), vertices through the shader JIT
  - GPU thread: the GSP's work runs on the thread with the GL context; its interrupts are sent when the CPU next idles or at VBlank, after the queue drains
  - Idle skip: ignore wakeup ticks already passed (the idle loop spun for whole GPU waits); leave the JIT when the next event is due; GL: skip unchanged fragment uniform uploads
  - panda3ds-bench: --dump writes each scene's last frame, --dump-frames a,b,c
  - RendererGL: readScreen() reads the composited screens from the screen FBO
  - JIT: constant reads from read-only code (literal pools), halt after an idle time skip so the scheduler runs at once
  - Banana speed work: PANDA3DS_BASELINE switch, a yield with nothing else to run skips to the next event, dynarmic's code cache 32 MB, bench --maps
  - GL textures: decode a tile at a time, per format (ETC1 a block at a time)
  - panda3ds-bench: a headless benchmark frontend (EGL surfaceless, scene markers from svcOutputDebugString)

